    26	    - title: "Compact space (Wikipedia)"
    27	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_space"
    28	    - title: "Cover (topology) (Wikipedia)"
    29	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cover_(topology)"
    30	    - title: "J. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., §26"
    31	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Munkres"
    32	    - title: "Stacks Project, Section 5.12: Quasi-compact spaces and maps"
    33	      url: "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0059"
    34	pipeline_run: null
    35	---
    36	## Definition
    37	
    38	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
    39	
    40	- An **open cover** of $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is a family
    41	  $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ of open sets with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$,
    42	  where $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
    43	- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$
    44	  that is itself an open cover.
    45	- A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \varnothing$ or
    46	  there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
    47	  $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
    48	  harmless.
    49	- $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite
    50	  subcover: for every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \varnothing$ and the
    51	  empty subfamily covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    52	  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
    53	  $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
    54	- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the subspace
    55	  $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact topological space, $\mathcal{T}_A$ being the
    56	  subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    57	
    58	**Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
    59	The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ and its own open
    60	sets, not about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do
    61	agree, but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
    62	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], and no item of this library may use
    63	the ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading makes
    64	compactness a property of the topological space $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ alone.
    65	Hence it is preserved when $A$ is embedded homeomorphically as a subspace, or
    66	when another ambient space induces the same topology on $A$; it need not be
    67	preserved if the induced topology changes. This is exactly the convention already fixed for
    68	metric spaces by [[def-metric-compactness]], and the agreement of that
    69	definition with this one is [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
    70	
    71	**The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
    72	it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. Every
    73	space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact too: given a cover, each $x_i$
    74	lies in some member, and finitely many members named in this way already cover.
    75	So every finite space is compact, whatever its topology, and in particular the
    76	discrete topology on a finite set is compact while the discrete topology on an
    77	infinite set is not ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
    78	
    79	**The finiteness convention.** "Finite" above is the listing form. It agrees with
    80	the definition of finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number
    81	([[def-countable]]), in both directions, and the agreement is the one already
    82	discharged in [[def-metric-compactness]]: a nonempty set equinumerous with
    83	$m \ge 1$ is listable, and a set listed as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ injects into
    84	$\sigma(n)$ by sending $x$ to the least $i \le n$ with $a_i = x$. Neither
    85	direction uses a choice principle; the second selects nothing, taking a least
    86	index instead.
    87	
    88	**Quasicompact is not used here.** Some authors, following Bourbaki, reserve
    89	*compact* for a space that is both compact in the above sense and Hausdorff, and
    90	call the open-cover condition alone *quasicompact*. This library follows the more
    91	widely adopted convention: **compact** means the open-cover condition and nothing
    92	more, and a Hausdorff hypothesis is always written out. The fork is recorded in
    93	[[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]].
    94	
    95	## Remarks
    96	
    97	**Why open covers rather than covers by arbitrary sets.** Nothing in the
    98	definition would break if $\mathcal{U}$ were allowed to consist of arbitrary
    99	subsets of $X$, but the resulting notion would be uninteresting: every space is
   100	covered by its singletons, and only a finite space would survive. Openness of the
   101	members is what makes the condition a genuine restriction, and it is what
   102	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]] has to keep track of when the
   103	ambient space changes.
   104	
   105	**A warning about the word "cover".** A family may cover $A \subseteq X$ without
   106	being a family of subsets of $A$: the members are open subsets of $X$ and their
   107	union merely contains $A$. That is the ambient reading, and it is a different
   108	statement from "$\mathcal{U}$ is an open cover of the space
   109	$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$", whose members are open subsets of $A$. Which of the two is
   110	meant is written out everywhere on this page.

