    40	---
    41	
    42	## Definition
    43	
    44	Let $(L, \le)$ be a **linearly ordered set** ([[def-partial-order]]): a poset in
    45	which any two elements are comparable. Write $<$ for the associated strict order.
    46	
    47	### Rays, intervals, and the order topology
    48	
    49	For $a \in L$ the **open rays** at $a$ are
    50	
    51	$$L_{<a} \;:=\; \{\, t \in L : t < a \,\}, \qquad L_{>a} \;:=\; \{\, t \in L : a < t \,\},$$
    52	
    53	and $\mathcal{S}_L := \{\, L_{<a} : a \in L \,\} \cup \{\, L_{>a} : a \in L \,\}$
    54	is the family of all of them. The **order topology** on $L$ is
    55	
    56	$$\mathcal{T}_{<} \;:=\; \langle \mathcal{S}_L \rangle,$$
    57	
    58	the topology generated by $\mathcal{S}_L$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]],
    59	[[def-topological-space]]). A **linearly ordered topological space** is a
    60	linearly ordered set carrying its order topology. For $a, b \in L$ write
    61	
    62	$$(a,b) := \{\, t \in L : a < t < b \,\}, \quad [a,b] := \{\, t \in L : a \le t \le b \,\},$$
    63	$$[a,b) := \{\, t \in L : a \le t < b \,\}, \quad (a,b] := \{\, t \in L : a < t \le b \,\},$$
    64	
    65	so that $(a,b) = L_{>a} \cap L_{<b}$.
    66	
    67	**A basis, and the obligation is discharged here.** By [[thm-basis-criterion]]
    68	claim 2 the intersections of finitely many members of $\mathcal{S}_L$ form a
    69	basis for $\mathcal{T}_{<}$. This library takes the empty intersection to be $L$,
    70	so $L$ itself is among them. An intersection of finitely many rays is computed by
    71	collecting the lower cuts and the upper cuts separately: since $\le$ is linear,
    72	a finite nonempty set of elements of $L$ has a greatest and a least member, so
    73	$L_{<a_1} \cap \dots \cap L_{<a_m} = L_{<a}$ with $a$ the least of the $a_i$, and
    74	$L_{>b_1} \cap \dots \cap L_{>b_k} = L_{>b}$ with $b$ the greatest of the $b_j$.
    75	Hence every finite intersection is $L$, an open ray, or an open interval
    76	$(b,a) = L_{>b} \cap L_{<a}$, and
    77	
    78	$$\mathcal{B}_L \;:=\; \{L\} \;\cup\; \mathcal{S}_L \;\cup\; \{\, (a,b) : a, b \in L \,\}$$
    79	
    80	is a basis for $\mathcal{T}_{<}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
    81	
    82	**What a basic neighbourhood of a point looks like.** Let $x \in L$. If $x$ is
    83	neither the least nor the greatest element of $L$ ([[def-maximal-element]]), then
    84	some $a < x$ and some $b > x$ exist and $x \in (a,b)$; if $x$ is least, the sets
    85	$L_{<b}$ with $b > x$ are the basic sets containing $x$ apart from $L$ itself;
    86	if $x$ is greatest, they are the sets $L_{>a}$ with $a < x$. These three cases
    87	are the only ones, and every proof below that argues at a point splits along
    88	them.
    89	
    90	**Order-convex sets.** A subset $C \subseteq L$ is **order-convex** when
    91	
    92	$$x, z \in C \text{ and } x \le w \le z \;\Longrightarrow\; w \in C .$$
    93	
    94	Every ray and every one of the four interval forms above is order-convex, by
    95	transitivity of $\le$; so are $\varnothing$, every singleton, and $L$.
    96	
    97	**A convention that is fixed once here.** A subset $C \subseteq L$ inherits two
    98	topologies that need not agree: the subspace topology from $(L, \mathcal{T}_{<})$
    99	([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) and the order topology of the restricted order on
   100	$C$. **In this library "a subspace of a linearly ordered topological space"
   101	always means the subspace topology**, and the phrase "the order topology of $C$"
   102	is written in full whenever the second is meant. The two do agree when $C$ is
   103	order-convex, which is the only case used here: for order-convex $C$ the trace
   104	$L_{<a} \cap C$ is $C$ if $a$ is above every element of $C$, is $\varnothing$ if
   105	$a$ is below or equal to every element of $C$, and is otherwise the ray
   106	$C_{<a}$ when $a \in C$, and $C_{<c}$ for any $c \in C$ above $a$ has the same
   107	trace description; in every case the trace of a subbasic set of $L$ is a
   108	subbasic set of $C$ or is $\varnothing$ or $C$, and conversely every ray of $C$
   109	is such a trace. The general statement, for a subset that is not order-convex, is
   110	**not asserted here**.
   111	
   112	### Order-density and the least upper bound property
   113	
   114	Let $(L, \le)$ be linearly ordered.
   115	
   116	- $L$ is **order-dense** (or **densely ordered**) when for all $x, y \in L$ with
   117	  $x < y$ there is $z \in L$ with $x < z < y$. Equivalently, no element of $L$
   118	  has an immediate successor above it: there is no pair $x < y$ with $(x,y) =
   119	  \varnothing$.
   120	- $L$ has the **least upper bound property** when every nonempty $S \subseteq L$
   121	  that has an upper bound in $L$ has a least upper bound in $L$
   122	  ([[def-upper-bound]]). A least upper bound is unique when it exists, by
   123	  antisymmetry: two of them bound each other, and antisymmetry of $\le$
   124	  ([[def-partial-order]]) forces them equal. We write $\sup S$ for it.
   125	
   126	**A linear continuum** is a linearly ordered set with **at least two elements**
   127	that is order-dense and has the least upper bound property.
   128	
   129	**The two-element requirement is not decoration.** Without it the empty ordered
   130	set and every one-point ordered set would qualify vacuously, and the theorems
   131	about linear continua elsewhere in this library would have degenerate instances
   132	whose statements say nothing. A linear continuum in the sense above is
   133	automatically infinite: two elements $x < y$ produce $z_1$ strictly between them,
   134	then $z_2$ strictly between $x$ and $z_1$, and so on, and each is new because the
   135	order is strict.
   136	
   137	**$\mathbb{R}$ is a linear continuum, and its order topology is its usual
   138	topology.** The order of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-real-order]], [[def-ordered-field]])
   139	is linear; $\mathbb{R}$ has at least two elements, namely $0$ and $1$; it is
   140	order-dense because $x < (x+y)/2 < y$ whenever $x < y$
   141	([[def-ordered-field]]); and it has the least upper bound property, which is
   142	exactly the completeness axiom ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],
   143	[[lem-sup-unique]], [[def-infimum]], [[def-bounded-set]]). Its order topology is
   144	the usual topology, that is the metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
   145	([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]],
   146	[[def-metrizable-space]]): the ball $B(x,r)$ is the interval $(x - r, x + r)$
   147	([[def-metric-ball]], [[def-interval]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]), which is a
   148	basic set of $\mathcal{T}_{<}$, so every set open for the metric is open for the
   149	order; and conversely $L_{<b}$ and $L_{>a}$ are open for the metric
   150	([[def-open-and-closed-in-r]]), so every subbasic set of $\mathcal{T}_{<}$ is
   151	metric-open and $\mathcal{T}_{<} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_{d_{\mathbb{R}}}$ by
   152	minimality of the generated topology ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]). The two
   153	topologies therefore coincide, and **no second topology on $\mathbb{R}$ is being
   154	introduced**.
   155	
   156	## Remarks
   157	
   158	- **The dictionary with the ordinal case.** [[def-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]]
   159	  puts a topology on an ordinal $\gamma$ using the initial segments $[0,\beta]$
   160	  and the half-open intervals $(\alpha,\beta]$ as a basis, and says in its own
   161	  body that this is the general order basis rewritten so that no case analysis is
   162	  needed. The two agree: $[0,\beta] = \gamma_{<\beta^{+}}$ when
   163	  $\beta^{+} \in \gamma$ and is $\gamma$ otherwise, and
   164	  $(\alpha,\beta] = \gamma_{>\alpha} \cap \gamma_{<\beta^{+}}$ under the same
   165	  proviso, so every basic set there is a finite intersection of rays here;
   166	  conversely $\gamma_{<\beta} = [0,\beta]\setminus\{\beta\}$ is the union of the
   167	  sets $[0,\xi]$ with $\xi < \beta$, and $\gamma_{>\alpha}$ is the union of the
   168	  sets $(\alpha,\beta]$ with $\alpha < \beta < \gamma$, so every ray here is a
   169	  union of basic sets there. The two topologies have the same open sets.
   170	
   171	- **The same dictionary for the rays presentation.** [[ex-order-topology]]
   172	  presents the order topology of a totally ordered set by exactly the subbasis
   173	  used above and identifies it with the usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$; the
   174	  present item repeats that identification because it is the one every later
   175	  proof quotes, and adds the order-convexity, order-density and least-upper-bound
   176	  vocabulary that the linear-continuum theorems need.
   177	
   178	- **Why the rays and not the intervals.** Taking only the open intervals $(a,b)$
   179	  as a basis fails whenever $L$ has a least or a greatest element: no interval
   180	  contains the least element unless some element sits below it. The rays repair
   181	  this without a case split, which is why they are the subbasis of record here.
   182	
   183	- **Order-density is not topological density.** [[def-dense-top]] calls a subset
   184	  $A \subseteq X$ dense when $\overline{A} = X$. Order-density is a property of
   185	  the ordered set itself, not of a subset, and the two words coincide only by
   186	  historical accident. Where both are in play this library writes *order-dense*
   187	  in full.

