    29	    - title: "J. Munkres, Topology, 2nd ed., §13"
    30	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Munkres"
    31	pipeline_run: null
    32	---
    33	
    34	## Definition
    35	
    36	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
    37	
    38	A family $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ is a **basis for $\mathcal{T}$** if
    39	every open set is a union of members of $\mathcal{B}$: for every
    40	$U \in \mathcal{T}$ there is $\mathcal{B}_U \subseteq \mathcal{B}$ with
    41	$U = \bigcup \mathcal{B}_U$. Equivalently, and this is the form used in proofs,
    42	
    43	$$\text{for every } U \in \mathcal{T} \text{ and every } x \in U \text{ there is } B \in \mathcal{B} \text{ with } x \in B \subseteq U .$$
    44	
    45	*The two forms say the same thing.* If every open $U$ is such a union and
    46	$x \in U$, then $x$ lies in one of the sets united, which is a member of
    47	$\mathcal{B}$ inside $U$. Conversely, if the displayed condition holds then
    48	$U = \bigcup \{\, B \in \mathcal{B} : B \subseteq U \,\}$, since each such $B$ is
    49	contained in $U$ and each $x \in U$ lies in one of them. Note that
    50	$\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$, so the empty open set is covered by the empty
    51	subfamily and needs no member of $\mathcal{B}$. The members of a basis are called
    52	**basic open sets**.
    53	
    54	**The topology generated by a family.** Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$
    55	be *any* family of subsets of $X$. Then
    56	
    57	$$\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle := \bigcap \{\, \mathcal{T}' : \mathcal{T}' \text{ is a topology on } X \text{ with } \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}' \,\}$$
    58	
    59	is a topology on $X$, it contains $\mathcal{S}$, and it is contained in every
    60	topology on $X$ that contains $\mathcal{S}$. It is called the **topology
    61	generated by $\mathcal{S}$**, and $\mathcal{S}$ is a **subbasis** for a topology
    62	$\mathcal{T}$ when $\mathcal{T} = \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$.
    63	
    64	*This is well posed, and the obligation is discharged here.* The collection being
    65	intersected is nonempty, because $\mathcal{P}(X)$ is a topology on $X$ containing
    66	$\mathcal{S}$; so the intersection is an intersection of a nonempty family of
    67	subsets of $\mathcal{P}(X)$ and is a set. It is a topology: $\varnothing$ and $X$
    68	lie in every topology on $X$, hence in the intersection, which is (T1); if
    69	$\mathcal{S}' \subseteq \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ then $\mathcal{S}'$ is a
    70	subfamily of each $\mathcal{T}'$ in the collection, so $\bigcup \mathcal{S}'$ lies
    71	in each $\mathcal{T}'$ and hence in the intersection, which is (T2); and the same
    72	argument with $U \cap V$ gives (T3). It contains $\mathcal{S}$ because every
    73	$\mathcal{T}'$ in the collection does, and it is contained in each such
    74	$\mathcal{T}'$ because an intersection is contained in each of its members. So
    75	$\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ is the coarsest topology on $X$ containing
    76	$\mathcal{S}$ ([[def-topological-space]]), and in particular it is uniquely
    77	determined by $\mathcal{S}$.
    78	
    79	**Neither notion is intrinsic to the family alone.** "$\mathcal{B}$ is a basis
    80	for $\mathcal{T}$" and "$\mathcal{S}$ is a subbasis for $\mathcal{T}$" are
    81	relations between a family and a topology, not properties of the family. The
    82	question of which families are a basis *for some* topology, and how the topology
    83	generated by a subbasis is computed from it, is settled by the next item.
    84	
    85	## Remarks
    86	
    87	- **Every topology is a basis for itself**, so a basis always exists; the point
    88	  of a basis is to be smaller and more explicit than $\mathcal{T}$, and the point
    89	  of a subbasis is to be smaller still at the cost of one round of finite
    90	  intersections.
    91	
    92	- **Two extremes of the generated topology.** $\langle \varnothing \rangle$ is
    93	  the indiscrete topology, since $\{\varnothing, X\}$ is a topology containing the
    94	  empty family and is contained in every topology. At the other end,
    95	  $\langle \mathcal{P}(X) \rangle = \mathcal{P}(X)$ is the discrete topology.
    96	
    97	- **Generation is monotone and idempotent.** If
    98	  $\mathcal{S}_1 \subseteq \mathcal{S}_2$ then every topology containing
    99	  $\mathcal{S}_2$ contains $\mathcal{S}_1$, so
   100	  $\langle \mathcal{S}_1 \rangle \subseteq \langle \mathcal{S}_2 \rangle$; and
   101	  $\langle \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle \rangle = \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$
   102	  because $\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ is itself a topology containing
   103	  $\mathcal{S}$. Both are used silently below.

