    31	---
    32	
    33	## Definition
    34	
    35	Let $(P, \le)$ be a poset ([[def-partial-order]]) and $S \subseteq P$.
    36	
    37	An element $u \in P$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for every
    38	$s \in S$.
    39	
    40	An element $u \in P$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**) of $S$ if $u$
    41	is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le v$ for every upper bound $v$ of $S$. When it
    42	exists we write $u = \sup S$.
    43	
    44	An element $u \in P$ is a **strict upper bound** of $S$ if $s < u$ for every
    45	$s \in S$.
    46	
    47	## Remarks
    48	
    49	- **A least upper bound is unique when it exists.** If $u$ and $u'$ are both
    50	  least upper bounds of $S$ then each is an upper bound and each is below the
    51	  other, so $u \le u'$ and $u' \le u$, whence $u = u'$ by antisymmetry
    52	  ([[def-partial-order]]). This is what makes the notation $\sup S$ legitimate.
    53	  Antisymmetry is not peculiar to this argument: the same two-inequality step
    54	  gives uniqueness of a greatest element ([[def-maximal-element]]), and it is
    55	  used essentially in [[thm-bourbaki-witt]], whose fixed point is obtained by
    56	  passing from $f(c) \le c$ and $c \le f(c)$ to $f(c) = c$. Drop antisymmetry
    57	  and it is the conclusion, not merely the notation, that goes: on two distinct
    58	  elements each below the other, every subset still has a least upper bound, yet
    59	  the map exchanging the two satisfies $x \le f(x)$ and has no fixed point.
    60	- **Every element of $P$ is an upper bound of the empty set**, vacuously.
    61	  Consequently $\sup \emptyset$, when it exists, is the least element of $P$.
    62	- An upper bound of $S$ need not belong to $S$, and $S$ may have many upper
    63	  bounds and no least one. In $\mathbb{Q}$ with its usual order, the set
    64	  $\{q \in \mathbb{Q} : q^2 < 2\}$ has upper bounds but no least upper bound.
    65	- In a poset, a strict upper bound is exactly an upper bound outside $S$. If
    66	  $u$ is strict then $u\notin S$, since $u<u$ is impossible. Conversely, if
    67	  $u$ is an upper bound and $u\notin S$, then every $s\in S$ satisfies
    68	  $s\le u$ and $s\ne u$, hence $s<u$. This distinction from an arbitrary upper
    69	  bound matters in [[thm-zorn]], where the argument must produce one outside
    70	  the chain.

